<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Direct distribution (DMG), NOT the Mac App Store.

      App Sandbox is ENABLED (beta.17). A non-sandboxed app that reads files in
      ~/Documents, ~/Desktop or ~/Downloads triggers a one-time macOS folder
      (TCC) consent prompt + spinner the first time. The sandbox replaces that
      with the system "powerbox": files the user picks via the open/save panel,
      drags onto the window, or launches via Finder are granted automatically —
      no folder prompt at all.

      `files.user-selected.read-write` is the grant the powerbox needs to let
      the editor read AND write the file the user picked (open, save, save-as,
      export). Every write in the active viewer targets either the picked file,
      a native save-panel result, or a user-picked output folder — all covered
      by the powerbox grant. Scratch/temp/recovery state lives in the app
      container, never beside the user's file. Reopening a *recent* file (a path
      from a previous session, with no fresh pick) is the only flow the grant
      does not cover; that is handled by security-scoped bookmarks captured via a
      native panel (see crates/macos-secure).

      `network.client` is needed for the updater and any opt-in network feature.
      The cs.* Hardened Runtime exceptions are what a WKWebView-based app needs
      (JIT for the JS engine) and keep the bundle notarization-ready for when a
      Developer ID signature is added. They only relax runtime restrictions and
      cannot introduce a sandbox denial.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
